# Generated from wrangler.jsonc by `wrangler types`. Regenerated by the
# pretypecheck/pretest hooks, so committing it would only ever add a stale
# 500 KB file that disagrees with the config.
worker-configuration.d.ts

# Throwaway measurement specs.
#
# Measuring against real workerd and real D1 is routine here — a step body gets
# 10 ms of CPU on Workers Free, D1 bills rows read, and "is this fast?" and
# "how much does this read?" are both answered by running something rather than
# by reasoning about it. Those specs live for one afternoon.
#
# Six rounds of chasing names: _bench_*, zz-scratch-probe, scratch-chain,
# qplan-probe, tmpq, aaa-measure. A whitelist was tried and reverted: ignoring
# every spec and naming the real ones means a NEW suite is silently never
# committed, which is invisible and costly, where a missed pattern here only
# makes the pre-push check complain, which is visible and free. Wrong failure
# to optimise for.
#
# So: the vocabulary people actually reach for, broadly. Real suites are named
# after what they test and contain none of these words.
#
# One pattern has already been removed for being too broad: `test/q*` was
# added for qcost and qplan-probe and silently excluded query-plan.spec.ts —
# the guard proving migrations 0007 and 0008 seek instead of scanning. The
# suite reported green with it switched off. `*cost*` and `*probe*` already
# caught both scratch files, so it bought nothing and cost the one test that
# proves the quota fixes work. Prefixes that could match a real suite are not
# worth it; match on the throwaway word instead.
test/_*
test/tmp*
test/zz*
test/aaa*
test/*scratch*
test/*probe*
test/*bench*
test/*measure*
test/*cost*
test/*-check.spec.ts

# Build output for the Node runner. Rebuilt from source on every run.
dist/
